{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-339424",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+1}{3{x}^{4}-x+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{7x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-339424\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"25\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> in the denominator, which is <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"26\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c34\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"27\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"